 succeeded in 101ms:
     1	# Larch Run Logs
     2	
     3	On a default `/implement --merge` run, a directory of structured log files is committed alongside the PR. These committed files are the single source of truth for full run content — voting tallies, rejected findings, version-bump reasoning, OOS observations, execution issues, run statistics, token/timing reports, and the session transcript. The tracking issue and PR body carry only slim projections.
     4	
     5	Exceptions: `--design-only --no-issues` and `repo_unavailable=true` produce no committed log at all (`$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/execution-issues.md` is the only audit trail and is removed at cleanup). `--design-only` runs with a tracking issue can still commit partial `larch-logs/implement/<RUN_ID>/` directories, but they stop before Step 7a and therefore omit Step-7a-only batches such as `session-transcript.jsonl`. Fork dry-run mode (`--forked`) does not create a tracking issue. In all cases, session-derived content in `larch-logs/` passes through secrets and tmpdir-path redaction, but redaction is best-effort — operators should avoid pasting sensitive content into `/implement` prompts.
     6	
     7	## Directory structure
     8	
     9	```
    10	larch-logs/
    11	  implement/
    12	    <RUN_ID>/
    13	      manifest.json
    14	      plan-goals-test.md
    15	      parent-issue.md
    16	      pre-review-head.txt
    17	      pre-review-untracked.txt
    18	      codex-impl-transcript.txt
    19	      codex-impl-transcript-prompt.txt
    20	      codex-commit-message.txt
    21	      codex-impl-manifest-raw.json
    22	      plan-review-tally.json
    23	      code-review-tally.json
    24	      review-findings-full.jsonl
    25	      version-bump-reasoning.md
    26	      final-summary.md
    27	      oos-issues.ndjson
    28	      run-statistics.md
    29	      token-report.json
    30	      timing-report.json
    31	      execution-issues.ndjson
    32	      session-transcript.jsonl
    33	      round-<N>/
    34	        findings.md
    35	        accepted-findings.md
    36	        rejected-findings.md
    37	        review-round-summary.md
    38	        review-summary.json
    39	        voting-tally.md
    40	        aggregator-validate.stderr / aggregator-dispatch.stderr (when the findings aggregator fails; committed under the round directory when `write-round` runs)
    41	        *-output.txt
    42	        *-output.txt.meta
    43	        *-output.txt.json
    44	  review/
    45	    <RUN_ID>/
    46	      manifest.json
    47	      review-context.md
    48	      review-panel-manifest.ndjson
    49	      review-findings.ndjson
    50	      review-tally.md
    51	      review-scout-manifest.json
    52	      review-round-summary.md
    53	```
    54	
    55	`<RUN_ID>` is the UUID assigned at the start of each `/implement` session. Batch payload files under a run directory are redacted for secrets and tmpdir paths before commit. `manifest.json` schema version 2 keeps `operator_cwd` / `operator_repo_root` only as stable redacted placeholders (`"<OPERATOR_CWD>"`, `"<REPO_ROOT>"`) so committed logs preserve schema shape without exposing operator-local absolute paths.
    56	
    57	`round-<N>/` directories are written by `larch-log.sh write-round` during
    58	`/implement` code review. They preserve the per-round reviewer and voter
    59	diagnostic artifacts that are otherwise lost with `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR` cleanup.
    60	Only registered artifact names are copied. `.meta` files have `CMD_JSON=...`
    61	removed when `CMD_JSON=` is the first non-whitespace token, included
    62	`*-output.txt.json` / `*-output-*.txt.json` sidecars have their top-level
    63	`.result` field removed, and all copied files still pass through the normal
    64	tmpdir and secrets redaction. This trimming is specific to the committed round
    65	artifacts; the session tmpdir may still hold raw sidecars for in-run retries.
    66	If JSON trimming fails, `write-round` fails closed instead of copying the raw
    67	sidecar into `larch-logs/`.
    68	
    69	`/review` uses the same `larch-logs/<skill>/<RUN_ID>/` layout when a run ID is provided. Review phase names are encoded in flat batch slugs, not subdirectories: `review-context` for gathered context, `review-panel-manifest` for launched slots, `review-findings` for collected finding records, `review-tally` for vote results, `review-scout-manifest` for dynamic-reviewer scout status, and `review-round-summary` for the human-readable round summary.
    70	
    71	## manifest.json
    72	
    73	Created by `scripts/larch-log.sh init` at Step 0.5 when the tracking issue is first resolved. Updated by `larch-log.sh manifest` calls throughout the run. Contains: skill name, run ID, operator CWD, operator repo root, tracking-issue number, PR number (once created), the run status last recorded in that manifest snapshot, and optional routing flags such as `coder_fallback=true` when omitted-`--coder` routing fell past Codex. Authoritative contract: `scripts/larch-log.md`.
    74	
    75	For current `/implement` runs, the committed manifest is normally an `"in-progress"` snapshot because the post-merge `"done"` update happens inside `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR` after the last log commit window. That is not an absolute invariant: older committed runs, tests, or manual/status-update flows can still produce committed manifests with `"done"` or other statuses. To assess completion, read `status` as one signal and correlate it with PR merge state plus the surrounding run-log artifacts.
    76	
    77	## Batch files
    78	
    79	### plan-goals-test.md
    80	
    81	**Mode**: replace (one file per run). **Written**: Step 1, after the design plan is finalized.
    82	
    83	Contains the implementation plan: goal statement, files to modify, approach, edge cases, and testing strategy. In normal mode the content comes from `/design`'s exported `plan.txt`; in quick mode it is the inline plan produced by the orchestrator.
    84	
    85	### parent-issue.md
    86	
    87	**Mode**: replace. **Written**: Step 1 and refreshed at the pre-bump flush when
    88	present.
    89	
    90	Tracking-issue sentinel with the adopted or created issue number and run ID.
    91	This is the session-scope idempotency source for tracking issue recovery.
    92	
    93	### pre-review-head.txt and pre-review-untracked.txt
    94	
    95	**Mode**: replace. **Written**: Step 5 round 1 initialization.
    96	
    97	`pre-review-head.txt` records the HEAD SHA before review starts.
    98	`pre-review-untracked.txt` records the untracked-file snapshot used by the
    99	review-change checks.
   100	
   101	### codex-impl-transcript.txt and related Codex setup files
   102	
   103	**Mode**: replace. **Written**: Step 7a pre-bump flush when present.
   104	
   105	`codex-impl-transcript.txt` is the external implementer transcript,
   106	`codex-impl-transcript-prompt.txt` is the prompt sidecar,
   107	`codex-commit-message.txt` is the redacted commit message consumed by the
   108	dispatcher, and `codex-impl-manifest-raw.json` is the pre-sanitized manifest
   109	copy retained for diagnosis. These files are optional because non-Codex or
   110	bailout paths may not produce them.
   111	
   112	### plan-review-tally.json
   113	
   114	**Mode**: replace (JSON object). **Written**: Step 1 tail, after the plan-review voting tally is exported.
   115	
   116	One JSON object per `/implement` session. The tally envelope shape is shared with
   117	`code-review-tally.json`: `schema_version`, `phase`, `batch`, `mode`, `rounds`,
   118	`accepted_count`, `rejected_count`, `exonerated_count`, `neutral_count`, and
   119	`body`. For plan review the extra counters are normally `0`. The `body` contains
   120	the plan-review voting outcome (accepted count, rejected count, round summaries)
   121	plus any rejected plan-review findings under a `## Rejected Plan Review Findings`
   122	sub-header. In quick mode the body contains `"Quick mode — no plan review voting."`.
   123	
   124	### code-review-tally.json
   125	
   126	**Mode**: replace (JSON object). **Written**: Step 5, after `/review` returns (normal mode) or the quick-mode review loop completes.
   127	
   128	One JSON object per `/implement` session with the same tally envelope fields as
   129	`plan-review-tally.json`. `exonerated_count` covers findings voted `exonerated`
   130	(valid but not worth implementing in this PR), `neutral_count` covers tie votes
   131	with no clear consensus (finding-level result from `classify_result()`), and
   132	`rejected_count` counts only findings where the panel voted strictly `rejected`
   133	(voted down). The body contains the code-review voting outcome and a round-by-round
   134	summary. It also includes rejected code-review findings under a
   135	`## Rejected Code Review Findings` sub-header — only findings with outcome
   136	`rejected` appear here. Exonerated and neutral findings are counted in the envelope
   137	but not listed separately.
   138	
   139	**Note**: `neutral_count` covers finding-level tied votes and is distinct from
   140	`JUDGE_ERROR`, which is a per-judge-per-finding state (the parser fallback when a
   141	voter's ballot did not contain a parseable vote line for that finding). `JUDGE_ERROR`
   142	appears in the per-finding vote breakdown table under the `JERR` column header but
   143	is not separately enumerated in the tally envelope counters.
